{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"rockbound-indistinct-sponge","creation_channel":"cli","description":"The completion verifier's context management grew by accretion across debugging sessions and its current shape was never operator-ratified. Operator ruling (2026-08-05, recorded verbatim): \"Going through plan review doesn't mean the user saw it or approved it. The design choice was an overreach, likely from a debugging session.\" This epic is the ratified rework. Central theme: the completion verifier's CONTEXT MANAGEMENT — what context each verification run receives, how AC verification is batched across runs, and what happens when context or budgets are exceeded.\n\nThe three defect/inefficiency classes this epic remediates, each with live production evidence:\n\n1. UNOPTIMIZED RECOVERY DESIGN (unratified fan-out). Bounded recovery runs one isolated evidence agent PER acceptance criterion (completion_recovery.py _recover). Measured on the live c9f7 shape (22 ACs, 121,147-char context): worst case 22 x 20 = 440 requests — 1.8x the primary's entire 240-request budget, for the FALLBACK path; 22 x 121k = 2.67M chars (~670k tokens) of context re-sends, effectively uncached because instructions place the criterion BEFORE the context (no shared prefix for provider prompt caching); sequential evidence runs at 25-90s each = 9-33 min added wall clock. Zero amortization for overlapping or trivially small ACs (\"make lint passes\" and \"make typecheck passes\" each re-send the full context and re-explore the repo from scratch). Replacement direction (operator-proposed): banked incremental verification — one run over ALL remaining ACs, per-criterion verdicts banked as they commit (e.g. a record_criterion_verdict tool), exhaustion spawns a successor scoped to the UNBANKED remainder only; a zero-progress successor is the typed failure. Cost scales with difficulty, not AC count. A deterministic backstop (exhausted/banked-placeholder criterion can never score met) carries over from the ed6d review's G6 finding regardless of design.\n\n2. CONTEXT LIMITS DURING COMPLETION VERIFICATION. A flat _MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS=100,000 recovery preflight bound refused c9f7 (121,147 chars) three times deterministically — the store is append-only so the context only grows, making --force-close the only exit (the exhaustion-is-not-a-verdict trap, third form). Second outgrowth of the same flat bound (d59e raised 24k -> 100k months earlier). Already filed and scoped as bug 8eb3-1f20-f395-4ea5 (two-ceiling fix: physical model-window ceiling + economic total-spend ceiling). The banked redesign in (1) further shrinks the economic multiplier from criteria-count to restart-count.\n\n3. WALL-CLOCK COST OF COLD-START EXPLORATION. The verifier receives ZERO file contents up front — only the fenced ticket context (gate_ops.py:144-160) — and spends its opening requests hunting: all four live-captured runaway loops were search_files repeats. Wall clock is round-trips x ~4-6s/request (Bedrock), not tokens (~99% cache-read). Already filed and scoped as task a9dd-bbc7-f549-4fc2: Phase 0 records the raw tool-fetch lists agents actually make; Phase 1 pre-loads bounded file_impact contents + referencing-commit diffs into verifier context (PRE-FETCH, never restrict — the full tool surface stays). Live A/B against recorded baselines (84-158 requests) with a >=20% request-count reduction target.\n\nPrior-art research (two compiled reports, 2026-08-05, session files research-ctx-mgmt.md / research-batch-bank.md, primary-source citations throughout) validates the banked design and binds four constraints: (a) record-as-you-go via a tool matches the convergent incremental-commit pattern (Claude Code TodoWrite, LangGraph put_writes); the tool must be IDEMPOTENT (refuse double-banking on retry) and schema-versioned. (b) Banked verdicts must live in an EXTERNALIZED store the successor run reads (not agent-session state), with successor scope passed explicitly as the remaining-criteria list. (c) Banked-PASS revision is unsolved community-wide: the finalizer must hold explicit DOWNGRADE authority over banked PASSes on cross-criterion contradiction, and the deterministic backstop (placeholder/exhausted can never score met) is mandatory. (d) The finalizer must receive UNCOMPRESSED per-criterion evidence (SWE-agent's 5000-char truncated-trajectory adjudicator is the documented hallucination failure mode). Additionally: provider prompt caching on Bedrock only works via the Converse API path — verify which client path our pydantic-ai runner uses BEFORE counting on cache-aware ordering (stable context prefix first, varying criterion last) for the cost math.\n\nConstraints carried over from ratified prior decisions (do not relitigate): nothing is ever elided from assembled context (elision is a signed-false-PASS vector — the d59e tests pin this); refusals stay visible and typed; budgets bound FAILURES, not capacity; the primary verifier's one-call fast path stays.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The banked/batched verification story is designed, planned through the plan-review gate, implemented, and live-proven: a multi-AC verification where one run exhausts completes via a remainder-scoped successor, with total requests strictly below the equivalent fan-out worst case for the same ticket shape (recorded on the story).\n- [ ] Bug 8eb3-1f20-f395-4ea5 (context-bound refusal) is closed through the live gate: the c9f7 shape (121,147 chars, 22 criteria) is admitted to recovery and the \"completion recovery context bound exceeded\" gate_error_v1 no longer reproduces.\n- [ ] Task a9dd-bbc7-f549-4fc2 (context pre-loading) is closed through the live gate with its Phase 0 overlap evidence and Phase 1 live A/B recorded (>=20% request reduction, verdict class unchanged).\n- [ ] A deterministic backstop ships: a criterion whose evidence is a placeholder/banked-exhausted entry can never be scored met by the finalizer — enforced in code after the finalizer returns, covered by a unit test that asserts the override/fail-closed behavior even when the finalizer returns met=true.\n- [ ] The epic records a design note (ticket comment or ADR) stating the ratified context-management contract: what the verifier is given, what it may fetch, how ACs are batched, and the two ceilings that bound recovery spend.\n- [ ] make lint and make typecheck pass on every landed change; the unit marker suite matches the pre-existing baseline throughout.\n\n## Testing\nEach child story/bug carries its own held-out RED-first unit oracle plus a live Bedrock proof before ship (session experiments, never in the unit suite), per the standing directive. Epic-level: the live proofs enumerated in the ACs above, recorded as ticket comments with request counts and verdicts.","id":"10ae-aa24-3fdc-4f0b","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Rework completion-verifier context management: batched AC verification, context ceilings, pre-loading"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785991666034187001,"uuid":"4e28c48a-3c9e-4819-8c2f-ee5a3a9590f3"}